    "user_prompt": "I am giving a workshop on Advanced Windsurf. I need you to take this repo and give me basically a play-by-play of a workshop that could last roughly 35-45 minutes long. Here is my Devin CLI workshop, so format it similar where you can come up with SIMPLE prompts for me to send into windsurf at different points. Basically, make the plan + script + prompts. Here are teh slides/general flow of what to follow:\n\n1.  **(No Title)**\n    \n      * April 2026\n      * Windsurf 201\n\n2.  **Purpose Built Models**\n    \n      * Use preference data from free users, which is a fundamental advantage that no other tool has access to.\n      * Models generate new information and start incorporating research agents like code execution and heuristic search.\n      * Model Personalization continues research into customer-specific models for lower maintenance and more deterministic results.\n      * Autocomplete and Command use context-aware training, focusing on Fill-in-the-middle (inline and multiline).\n      * Chat uses fast, context-aware proprietary models coupled with generic large LLMs (GPT-4, Claude Sonnet, Gemini, etc.).\n      * Task-Specific Foundation Models training continues for models that are lower latency and higher quality than larger general-purpose models.\n      * Model Optionality leverages general purpose foundational models as alternative backends where relevant.\n      * SWE 1.5 & SWE grep are Speed-Based Foundation Models optimized for speed (SWE 1.5 is 13x faster than Claude Sonnet 4.5) and match retrieval capabilities of frontier coding models.\n\n3.  **Fast Context**\n    \n      * SWE-grep provides Order-of-Magnitude Speed, retrieving context up to 20\u00d7 faster than traditional search.\n      * It offers Noise-Free Precision by returning only the most relevant file and line ranges, which reduces context pollution.\n      * SWE-grep-mini achieves Enterprise-Grade Scale, peaking at \\~2,800 tokens/sec for large-repo queries.\n\n4.  **DeepWiki for Windsurf**\n    \n      * Provides Instant Knowledge in Context, allowing users to hover and click to surface deep explanations of functions, classes, or files inside Windsurf.\n      * Accelerates Onboarding & Ramp for new developers by helping them understand codebases faster.\n      * Bridges Code & Documentation by pushing insights seamlessly into Cascade, keeping documentation fresh.\n      * Reduces Debug & Review Cycles by minimizing back-and-forth and rework across teams.\n\n5.  **Deep Codebase Understanding**\n    \n      * Features 4x More Accurate Retrieval with a proprietary approach that outperforms embedding search while maintaining low latency.\n      * DeepWiki Innovation automatically indexes repos and generates wikis with architecture diagrams, source links, and code summaries.\n      * Offers Seamless Access through a unified view of documentation, diagrams, and references across all repositories.\n\n6.  **Codemaps**\n    \n      * Code Intelligence Maps visualize code execution order and relationships, turning complex repos into navigable hierarchies.\n      * Offers Collaborative Context via shareable, browser-viewable maps that instantly align teams on structure and logic.\n      * Enables AI-Ready Integration by feeding contextual code insights directly into Cascade for smarter AI reasoning.\n\n7.  **Hooks & Skills**\n    \n      * Hooks provide Programmatic Controls for executing custom commands automatically when Cascade is running.\n      * Full Coverage includes events like user prompts, Cascade reading files, and Cascade writing to files.\n      * Logging allows hooks to be used to log all actions, including prompts, tool calls, and MCP usage.\n      * Guidance and Security features allow you to programmatically apply standards to code written by Cascade and block certain files from being read.\n\n8.  **Git Worktrees**\n    \n      * Parallelization is a tool for spinning up multiple Cascade sessions that can run on different worktrees.\n      * Composability focuses on enabling developers to run and merge agentic changes that might otherwise have conflicts.\n      * Merging allows you to click \"merge\" to incorporate file changes made by Cascade in the worktree back into your main workspace.\n\n9.  **Queued Messages**\n    \n      * Allows Multiple Messages to be queued within the same conversation if you already know your next prompt.\n      * Facilitates Conversation Management, making it easier to handle multiple conversations simultaneously.\n\n10. **(No Title)**\n    \n      * **Adaptive Mode:** A new model mode that routes Cascade to use the optimal model for a given task.\n      * **Key features:** Determines the correct model, uses the existing model list for enterprise, enables adaptive usage of credits, and lets users see the model used in response statistics.\n      * **What it enables:** Reduces the cognitive load of switching between the right models.\n\n11. **(No Title)**\n    \n      * **Agent Command Center:** A new command center to manage all agents running on the Cognition Platform.\n      * **Key features:** Easily manage multiple agent \"sessions\" at once, view a kanban interface for active/blocked/ready-for-review agents, and use an interface tailored to agent-first development.\n      * **What it enables:** Developing and merging code faster while maintaining clear oversight of development.\n\n12. **(No Title)**\n    \n      * **Spaces:** A way to organize all work done across multiple agents, where all sessions within a space share context.\n      * **Key features:** New sessions automatically share context, allows easy switching between tasks in different sessions, and enables using different types of agents within a space.\n      * **What it enables:** Cross-agent work and better organization of agent context.\n\n13. **(No Title)**\n    \n      * **Support Engineering:** Windsurf has a dedicated support engineering team in the USA & India.\n      * Enterprise customers are prioritized by default; you must be logged in when filing a ticket.\n      * If you encounter bugs, first file a Support ticket and include your diagnostics log.\n      * Post in the Slack channel only after filing a ticket with the Support team.\n      * Support is available at [windsurf.com/support](windsurf.com/support).\n\n\nHere is teh script for devin CLI:\n\nWe are the team behind Devin and windsurf, and this workshop is a part of a series of workshops where we get you guys set up with all of our tools. Today I will be showing you guys the Devin CLI and how you can use it to automate parts of your software development workflow\n\nSlides (3-4 min)\nModel Agnostic\nNew models are coming out every day\nGpt models are better than anthropic models, anthropic models become better than gpt models\nWe allow you to ALWAYS use the best model, but also use proprietary models\nDevin CLI, just like Windsurf, runs locally. However the terminal is a lot more lightweight, because it runs directly in the terminal\nDemo (25 min)\nInstall devin\nhttps://devin.ai/terminal\nRun devin\nGive me a 3 sentence summary of the codebase\nAs you can see, it navigates the codebase and runs commands to get a better understanding\n\n\nAs you can see, by default a lot of the terminal commands it will ask me for permission for. As you work with Devin CLI, you will be able to whitelist these commands for the future by clicking Allow. \nPermissions handling - /mode\nNormal mode will ask us permission to run commands, will constantly be prompting you\nAccept edits mode will automatically make edits to files, but will ask for permission to run terminal commands\nBypass mode will not ask any permissions, so it maximizes speed at the risk of running a locally in your codebase\nFor the sake of the meeting, I will be running in bypass mode\nNow that we have configured the mode, I\u2019m going to kick off Devin to actually start making changes\nAll critical alerts must be in yellow and not in red for the dashboard\nDevin went through and made the changes successfully\nContext preservation when resuming sessions:\ndevin -c (continue most recent)\n\n\nMake the primary color of the headers be dark green instead of blue\nIt just made this massive change very quickly, with no human intervention\nThis was Devin just going through and making changes to the codebase. Standard coding agent, similar to cascade, however this is going to be running in your terminal directly.\nThis entire time, I have been using gpt 5.5, one of the things that makes devin specifically unique, is his ability to change models\n/models opus 4.7\nmake it so that the actions in the table on the dashboard have tooltips\nAlso made that change fully via prompting.\n/model adaptive\nmake sure that the state of the opened panels is saved across refresh \nAllow you to manage the context\n/context\n/new\nThroughout this, I will constantly be refreshing the context window\nUse /plan mode for more complex tasks\n/plan change the filtering on the inbox page to not be tab based, but rather hidden behind a menu \nRelatively ambiguous task, not sure how it plans to implement it. Approve the plan\nBreak - So far we\u2019ve seen using it to \nmake changes and ask questions to the codebase\nSwap models and adaptive mode\nPlan and complete a more complex task.\nALL WITHOUT OPENING A SINGLE FILE\nI also briefly went over context management, with \nAGENTS.md\nPrompt that is always injected into the context window of the agent\nPutting rules\nSkills\ndevin skills list\nIn the codebase they are represented as markdown files\nDifference between skills and agents, skills are more for extra capabilities, agents is a really minimal file for rules and a system prompt for Devin\nI know i just ran devin skills list, If you are ever unsure what commands you can alway just run devin -h and you will get a wide range of the different skills devin has\nMCP\nFor those of you guys who are not familiar with MCP, this is essentially a protocol for your Devin for CLI to be able to connect to external integration such as Github, Grafana, or Atlassian. So today, what I\u2019ll show you is the fact that I have Devin MCP set up to actually connect with my Atlassian.\nAsk devin to add the Atlassian MCP\nAsk me to log-in to my atlassian\nnow is connected to my Jira\nwhat are 3 tasks assigned to me right now in jira?\nWhat are 3 tasks in a todo status in jira?\nPut these 3 tasks in an in-progress status \nThats MCP, integrate with external tools\nNow I\u2019ll show you subagents\nSwap to /swe 1.6 fast\nTake these three tasks and spin off individual sub agents to tackle each task\nFully running in the background\nConsistently check on the status, the parent has access to their status\n*hit the down arrow to see the sub agents* \nto view the status of the agents working\nMark these tasks as complete in atlassian\nHow to use the CLI, how to tackle complex tasks with the CLI, context management + model swapping, MCPs, and subagents\nONE LAST THING that I\u2019d like to show you guys is /handoff. \n/new + /bypass\n/plan make a plan to implement dark mode\nreject\n/handoff implement dark mode\nCould close my laptop, go to sleep, and the agent will still be working on the task in the cloud.\nCould also spin off 30 of these or a supppperrr complex task before I go to bed, and devin will complete it end to end. \nWake up with a full PR\nWhile Devin CLI is amazing for local dev, you can do a /handoff and have the task be fully completed in devin. Right now this is only rolled out to part of cisco, so if you guys are interested in the handoff functionality, please feel free to reach out to Hannah and I and we will follow up with you guys\nDevin is a super lightweight agent that runs in your terminal\nToday we went over \nBasic development\nComplex development\nModel switching\nContext management\nMCP integration\nSub agents\nHanding off to Devin in the cloud\nIf you guys have any additional questions, now is our chance to go over them.\n\n"
