    "response": "*Rules used for this response:*\n- (Always On) Triggered Rule: AGENTS.md\n\n### Planner Response\n\nLet me explore the repo to understand the codebase and craft realistic prompts.\n\n*Found code context*\n\n*Viewed file:///Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/.windsurf/hooks.json*\n\n*Viewed file:///Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/.windsurf/hooks/log_action.py*\n\n*Viewed file:///Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/AGENTS.md*\n\n*Listed directory file:///Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/.windsurf/skills*\n\n*Listed directory file:///Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/demos-coghealth-ehr-web/src/components*\n\n*Listed directory file:///Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/demos-coghealth-ehr-web/src/components/ui*\n\n*Listed directory file:///Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/demos-coghealth-ehr-web/src*\n\n*Viewed file:///Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/demos-coghealth-ehr-web/src/index.css*\n\n*Listed directory file:///Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/demos-coghealth-ehr-web/src/pages*\n\n*Listed directory file:///Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/.windsurf/skills/frontend-design*\n\n*Viewed file:///Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/.windsurf/skills/frontend-design/SKILL.md*\n\n*Viewed file:///Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/demos-coghealth-ehr-web/tests/dashboard.test.ts*\n\n### Planner Response\n\nNow I have a thorough understanding of the repo. Here's the full workshop script:\n\n---\n\n# Windsurf 201 \u2014 Advanced Windsurf Workshop Script\n\n**Duration:** 35\u201345 minutes  \n**Codebase:** CogHealth EHR \u2014 a React/Tailwind electronic health records app with a retro Windows XP desktop aesthetic.\n\n---\n\n## Part 1 \u2014 Slides: What Powers Windsurf (5\u20137 min)\n\n### Slide 1: Windsurf 201 Title\n> *\"We are the team behind Devin and Windsurf. This workshop is part of a series where we get you set up with all of our tools. Today I'll be going deeper on Windsurf \u2014 specifically the advanced features that make it the most powerful AI IDE on the market.\"*\n\n### Slide 2: Purpose Built Models\n> *\"Unlike other tools, Windsurf is built on purpose-built models. We train on preference data from millions of free users \u2014 that's a fundamental advantage nobody else has. Our models like SWE 1.5 are 13\u00d7 faster than Claude Sonnet 4.5 and match the retrieval capabilities of frontier coding models. You'll see this speed in action today.\"*\n\n### Slide 3: Fast Context (SWE-grep)\n> *\"SWE-grep is our proprietary context retrieval engine. It retrieves relevant context up to 20\u00d7 faster than traditional search, returning only the most relevant file and line ranges \u2014 no noise. SWE-grep-mini peaks at about 2,800 tokens/sec for large-repo queries. When Cascade searches through the codebase, this is what's powering it under the hood.\"*\n\n### Slide 4: DeepWiki for Windsurf\n> *\"DeepWiki gives you instant knowledge in context \u2014 hover and click to surface deep explanations of functions, classes, or files. It accelerates onboarding, bridges the gap between code and documentation, and reduces debug & review cycles. I'll show you how this works in a second.\"*\n\n### Slide 5: Deep Codebase Understanding\n> *\"Our retrieval is 4\u00d7 more accurate than embedding search while maintaining low latency. DeepWiki automatically indexes repos and generates wikis with architecture diagrams, source links, and code summaries.\"*\n\n### Slide 6: Codemaps\n> *\"Codemaps visualize code execution order and relationships \u2014 turning complex repos into navigable hierarchies. They're shareable, browser-viewable, and feed contextual insights directly into Cascade for smarter AI reasoning.\"*\n\n**Transition:** *\"Alright, that's enough slides. Let me switch over to the actual IDE and show you all of this in action.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Part 2 \u2014 Demo: Fast Context & Codebase Understanding (3\u20134 min)\n\n### \ud83d\udda5\ufe0f Setup\nOpen Windsurf with the `workshop` repo. Have the app running (`npm run dev` in `demos-coghealth-ehr-web`) and a browser tab showing the EHR dashboard at `localhost:5173`.\n\n### Prompt 1 \u2014 Codebase Summary\n> **Mode:** Ask  \n\n```\nGive me a 3 sentence summary of this codebase\n```\n\n**Talk track while it runs:**\n> *\"Watch how fast it navigates the entire repo. This is SWE-grep in action \u2014 it's not doing a naive file-by-file read. It's intelligently identifying the most relevant files and line ranges. Notice it found the main App.tsx, the page components, the services layer, the types \u2014 all in seconds.\"*\n\n**After result:**\n> *\"So it correctly identified this as a CogHealth EHR application \u2014 an electronic health records system built with React and Tailwind. It found the dashboard, patient search, scheduling, labs, vitals \u2014 everything. And it did that without me pointing it to any specific file.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Part 3 \u2014 AGENTS.md / Context Management (2\u20133 min)\n\n### Show AGENTS.md\nOpen `@/Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/AGENTS.md` in the editor.\n\n**Talk track:**\n> *\"Before we start making changes, let me show you something important \u2014 AGENTS.md. This is a file that is always injected into Cascade's context window. Think of it as permanent instructions for the AI.\"*\n\n> *\"Ours says: prefer small visible UI changes, reuse existing React/Tailwind/lucide patterns, keep components compact, don't add dependencies, and validate with `npm run build`. Every single prompt I send today, Cascade will follow these rules automatically. This is how you standardize AI behavior across your team.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Part 4 \u2014 Simple Code Change (3\u20134 min)\n\n### Prompt 2 \u2014 Visual Change\n> **Mode:** Code  \n\n```\nAll critical alerts on the dashboard must use a yellow/amber warning style instead of red. Update the CSS class ehr-alert-critical in index.css.\n```\n\n**Talk track while it runs:**\n> *\"This is a simple, targeted change. Watch how Cascade goes straight to the right file \u2014 it knows the CSS classes are in index.css because of the codebase understanding we talked about.\"*\n\n**After result \u2014 refresh browser:**\n> *\"There it is. The critical alerts banner on the dashboard is now yellow/amber instead of red. One prompt, one file changed, exactly what we asked for. Cascade respected our AGENTS.md rule of making small, visible UI changes.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Part 5 \u2014 Model Switching & Adaptive Mode (2\u20133 min)\n\n**Talk track (show the model selector dropdown in Windsurf):**\n> *\"One of the things that makes Windsurf unique is model optionality. Right here in the UI, I can switch between models \u2014 Claude Sonnet, GPT-4, SWE 1.5, and more.\"*\n\n> *\"But here's the killer feature: Adaptive Mode. When I select Adaptive, Windsurf automatically routes each task to the optimal model. Simple CSS change? It'll use a fast model. Complex refactor? It'll use a frontier model. You don't have to think about it \u2014 it reduces the cognitive load of switching models and optimizes your credit usage.\"*\n\n**Switch to Adaptive mode in the UI.**\n\n---\n\n## Part 6 \u2014 Targeted Feature Addition (4\u20135 min)\n\n### Prompt 3 \u2014 Add Tooltips\n> **Mode:** Code  \n\n```\nAdd descriptive tooltips to the action buttons in the inbox table on the dashboard. The View button should say \"Open patient chart\", the Mark Read button should say \"Mark as read\", and the Flag button should say \"Toggle flag\".\n```\n\n**Talk track while it runs:**\n> *\"This is a slightly more involved task \u2014 Cascade needs to find the inbox table in DashboardPage.tsx, locate the action buttons, and update the title attributes. Watch how it navigates directly to the right section of an 800+ line file.\"*\n\n**After result \u2014 refresh browser, hover over action buttons:**\n> *\"Hover over any action button in the inbox \u2014 you can see the tooltips. Cascade found the exact three buttons, updated the titles, and didn't touch anything else in this massive file. That's the precision of SWE-grep context.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Part 7 \u2014 Hooks Deep Dive (5\u20137 min)\n\n**Talk track:**\n> *\"Now let me show you one of the most powerful features for enterprise teams \u2014 Hooks.\"*\n\n### Show hooks.json\nOpen `@/Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/.windsurf/hooks.json` in the editor.\n\n> *\"Hooks are programmatic controls that execute custom commands automatically when Cascade is running. Look at this \u2014 we have two hooks configured:*\n> - *`pre_user_prompt` \u2014 fires before every prompt I send*\n> - *`post_cascade_response` \u2014 fires after every Cascade response*\n> \n> *Both run the same Python script.\"*\n\n### Show log_action.py\nOpen `@/Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/.windsurf/hooks/log_action.py` in the editor.\n\n> *\"This script logs every single action \u2014 file reads, file writes, terminal commands, MCP calls, user prompts, Cascade responses \u2014 all to an audit log. This is huge for compliance, security, and governance.\"*\n\n### Show the audit log\nOpen `@/Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/.windsurf/hooks/logs/cascade_audit.log`.\n\n> *\"Here's the actual log. Every prompt I've sent today, every file Cascade read, every edit it made \u2014 all captured with timestamps and full data. For regulated industries like healthcare \u2014 which is exactly what this EHR app is \u2014 this is a game changer.\"*\n\n**Talk track on other hook use cases:**\n> *\"Hooks go beyond logging. You can:*\n> - *Block Cascade from reading sensitive files (like `.env` or secrets)*\n> - *Enforce coding standards \u2014 run a linter after every file write*\n> - *Auto-run tests after changes*\n> - *Log all MCP usage for security audits*\n> \n> *Full coverage across the entire agent lifecycle.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Part 8 \u2014 Queued Messages (2\u20133 min)\n\n**Talk track:**\n> *\"Here's a productivity tip \u2014 Queued Messages. If you already know your next prompt while Cascade is still working, you can type it in and queue it. It'll execute as soon as the current task finishes.\"*\n\n### Prompt 4 + Prompt 5 (queued)\n> **Mode:** Code  \n\nSend the first prompt:\n```\nAdd a \"last updated\" timestamp text at the bottom of the Unsigned Notes panel on the dashboard that shows \"Last checked: \" followed by the current time\n```\n\nWhile Cascade is working, immediately type and queue:\n```\nAdd the same \"last updated\" timestamp to the Pending Orders panel\n```\n\n**Talk track:**\n> *\"I just sent two prompts \u2014 the second one is queued and waiting. This is great when you're on a roll and know exactly what you want done. No waiting around, no context switching. Cascade handles them sequentially.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Part 9 \u2014 Skills (2\u20133 min)\n\n**Talk track:**\n> *\"Skills are reusable capabilities you can give to Cascade. They're represented as markdown files in your repo.\"*\n\n### Show the skills directory\nOpen `@/Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/.windsurf/skills/frontend-design/SKILL.md` in the editor.\n\n> *\"This [frontend-design](file:///Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/.windsurf/skills/frontend-design:0:0-0:0) skill tells Cascade how to approach UI design \u2014 use bold aesthetics, avoid generic AI slop, match implementation to the vision. When Cascade detects a frontend task, it automatically activates this skill.\"*\n\n> *\"We also have a [pdf](file:///Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/.windsurf/skills/pdf:0:0-0:0) skill for working with PDF files. You can create custom skills for anything \u2014 API patterns, testing conventions, your company's design system. Think of skills as specialized knowledge modules that make Cascade smarter for your specific workflow.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Part 10 \u2014 Git Worktrees (3\u20134 min)\n\n**Talk track:**\n> *\"Git Worktrees solve a fundamental problem with AI agents \u2014 parallelization. Normally, if Cascade is making changes to your code, you can't work on something else in the same branch without conflicts.\"*\n\n> *\"With worktrees, you can spin up multiple Cascade sessions, each working on a different worktree \u2014 a separate working copy of your repo. They can all run in parallel without stepping on each other.\"*\n\n> *\"When a worktree session finishes, you click 'Merge' to incorporate the file changes back into your main workspace. It's composability for agentic development.\"*\n\n### Prompt 6 \u2014 Demonstrate worktree-worthy task\n> **Mode:** Code (in a new worktree session if available)\n\n```\nAdd a small \"Quality Metrics\" summary card to the right sidebar of the dashboard that shows 3 hardcoded stats: \"Notes signed on time: 92%\", \"Orders completed: 87%\", \"Patient satisfaction: 4.6/5\"\n```\n\n**Talk track:**\n> *\"This is exactly the kind of task you'd spin off into a worktree \u2014 an independent feature that doesn't touch the same code as what you're working on. Cascade adds the component, you review it, click merge, and it's in your main workspace.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Part 11 \u2014 Wrap-Up Slides (4\u20135 min)\n\n### Slide 10: Adaptive Mode\n> *\"We already saw this in action \u2014 Adaptive Mode routes Cascade to the optimal model for each task. It uses our existing model list for enterprise, enables adaptive credit usage, and lets you see which model was used in the response stats.\"*\n\n### Slide 11: Agent Command Center\n> *\"The Agent Command Center is a new interface to manage all agents running on the Cognition Platform. You get a kanban view of active, blocked, and ready-for-review agents. It's designed for agent-first development \u2014 managing multiple sessions at once.\"*\n\n### Slide 12: Spaces\n> *\"Spaces let you organize all work across multiple agents. All sessions within a space share context \u2014 so if one agent learns something about your codebase, the others benefit. You can switch between tasks, use different types of agents within a space. It enables true cross-agent collaboration.\"*\n\n### Slide 13: Support\n> *\"If you encounter bugs or issues: first, file a support ticket at windsurf.com/support. Include your diagnostics log. Enterprise customers are prioritized. Post in Slack only after filing a ticket. We have dedicated support engineering teams in the USA and India.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Closing (1\u20132 min)\n\n> *\"Let me recap what we covered today:*\n> 1. **Fast Context / SWE-grep** \u2014 20\u00d7 faster context retrieval, noise-free precision\n> 2. **AGENTS.md** \u2014 permanent context rules for your team\n> 3. **Simple & complex code changes** \u2014 all via natural language prompts\n> 4. **Model switching & Adaptive Mode** \u2014 always use the best model\n> 5. **Hooks** \u2014 logging, security, governance, full lifecycle coverage\n> 6. **Queued Messages** \u2014 no more waiting between prompts\n> 7. **Skills** \u2014 reusable knowledge modules for specialized tasks\n> 8. **Git Worktrees** \u2014 parallel agentic development with clean merges\n> \n> *All without manually navigating through a single file. If you have questions, now's the time.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference: All Prompts\n\n| # | Prompt | Feature Demonstrated |\n|---|--------|---------------------|\n| 1 | `Give me a 3 sentence summary of this codebase` | Fast Context / SWE-grep |\n| 2 | `All critical alerts on the dashboard must use a yellow/amber warning style instead of red. Update the CSS class ehr-alert-critical in index.css.` | Simple code change, AGENTS.md |\n| 3 | `Add descriptive tooltips to the action buttons in the inbox table on the dashboard...` | Targeted feature, model routing |\n| 4 | `Add a \"last updated\" timestamp text at the bottom of the Unsigned Notes panel...` | Queued messages (first) |\n| 5 | `Add the same \"last updated\" timestamp to the Pending Orders panel` | Queued messages (second) |\n| 6 | `Add a small \"Quality Metrics\" summary card to the right sidebar of the dashboard...` | Git Worktrees |"
