ding models. You'll see this speed throughout today's demo.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 3. Fast Context \u2014 SLIDE + DEMO (3\u20134 min)\n\n### Slide talk track:\n> *\"SWE-grep provides order-of-magnitude speed \u2014 retrieving context up to 20\u00d7 faster than traditional search. It returns only the most relevant file and line ranges, which reduces context pollution. SWE-grep-mini peaks at about 2,800 tokens/sec for large-repo queries. Let me show you.\"*\n\n### Prompt 1 \u2014 Demonstrate Fast Context\n> **Mode:** Ask\n\n```\nGive me a 3 sentence summary of this codebase\n```\n\n**While it runs:**\n> *\"Watch how fast it navigates the entire repo. This is SWE-grep \u2014 it's not doing a naive file-by-file read. It's intelligently identifying the most relevant files and line ranges. Notice it found App.tsx, the page components, services, types \u2014 all in seconds.\"*\n\n**After result:**\n> *\"It correctly identified this as a CogHealth EHR application built with React and Tailwind \u2014 dashboard, patient search, scheduling, labs, vitals \u2014 without me pointing it to a single file. That's noise-free precision.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 4. DeepWiki for Windsurf \u2014 SLIDE + DEMO (3\u20134 min)\n\n### Slide talk track:\n> *\"DeepWiki gives you instant knowledge in context. You can hover and click to surface deep explanations of functions, classes, or files directly inside Windsurf. It accelerates onboarding for new developers, bridges code and documentation by pushing insights into Cascade, and reduces debug and review cycles.\"*\n\n### Live Demo \u2014 Show DeepWiki in the IDE\nOpen [DashboardPage.tsx](file:///Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/demos-coghealth-ehr-web/src/pages/DashboardPage.tsx:0:0-0:0). Hover over a function/class to show DeepWiki popover.\n\n> *\"See this? I hover over `DashboardPage` and get a deep explanation \u2014 what it does, how it works, its dependencies. A new developer joining this project could understand this 800-line component instantly. And I can click to push this context directly into Cascade.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 5. Deep Codebase Understanding \u2014 SLIDE + DEMO (3\u20134 min)\n\n### Slide talk track:\n> *\"Our retrieval is 4\u00d7 more accurate than embedding search while maintaining low latency. DeepWiki automatically indexes repos and generates wikis with architecture diagrams, source links, and code summaries. All accessible through a unified view.\"*\n\n### Prompt 2 \u2014 Show deep retrieval\n> **Mode:** Ask\n\n```\nWhat is the relationship between the patient service layer and the dashboard page? How does data flow from the API to the UI?\n```\n\n**While it runs:**\n> *\"This question requires understanding multiple files and their relationships \u2014 the service layer, the types, the dashboard component. Watch how Cascade pulls together context from across the codebase to give a coherent answer.\"*\n\n**After result:**\n> *\"It traced the entire data flow \u2014 from `patientService.search()` to the dashboard's `useEffect` hook, through the mapping functions, into the UI state. That's deep codebase understanding, not just keyword matching.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 6. Codemaps \u2014 SLIDE (1\u20132 min)\n\n### Slide talk track:\n> *\"Codemaps visualize code execution order and relationships \u2014 turning complex repos into navigable hierarchies. They're shareable and browser-viewable, so you can instantly align teams on structure and logic. And they feed contextual code insights directly into Cascade for smarter AI reasoning.\"*\n\n> *\"Think of Codemaps as the visual counterpart to what SWE-grep does programmatically \u2014 making the structure of your code explicit and accessible.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 7. Hooks & Skills \u2014 SLIDE + DEMO (5\u20137 min)\n\n### Slide talk track:\n> *\"Hooks are programmatic controls for executing custom commands automatically when Cascade is running. They have full coverage \u2014 events like user prompts, Cascade reading files, and writing to files. You can use them for logging all actions including prompts, tool calls, and MCP usage. And for guidance and security \u2014 programmatically applying standards to code and blocking certain files from being read.\"*\n\n### Demo \u2014 Show the existing hooks setup\n\nOpen `@/Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/.windsurf/hooks.json` in the editor.\n\n> *\"Look at this \u2014 we have two hooks configured: `pre_user_prompt` fires before every prompt I send, and `post_cascade_response` fires after every response. Both run this Python script.\"*\n\nOpen `@/Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/.windsurf/hooks/log_action.py` in the editor.\n\n> *\"This script logs every action to an audit log \u2014 file reads, writes, terminal commands, MCP calls, prompts, responses \u2014 everything with timestamps and full data.\"*\n\nOpen `@/Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/.windsurf/hooks/logs/cascade_audit.log`.\n\n> *\"Here's the audit log from today's session. Every prompt I've sent, every file Cascade read \u2014 all captured. For regulated industries like healthcare \u2014 which is exactly what this EHR app is \u2014 this is a game changer for compliance.\"*\n\n> *\"Hooks go beyond logging \u2014 you can block reads to sensitive files, enforce linting after writes, auto-run tests, log MCP usage for security audits. Full lifecycle coverage.\"*\n\n### Demo \u2014 Show Skills\n\nOpen `@/Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/.windsurf/skills/frontend-design/SKILL.md`.\n\n> *\"Skills are reusable knowledge modules \u2014 markdown files in your repo. This [frontend-design](file:///Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/.windsurf/skills/frontend-design:0:0-0:0) skill tells Cascade how to approach UI work. We also have a [pdf](file:///Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/.windsurf/skills/pdf:0:0-0:0) skill. You can create custom skills for API patterns, testing conventions, your company's design system.\"*\n\n### Demo \u2014 Show AGENTS.md\n\nOpen `@/Users/wesconvery/dev/cog-gtm/workshop/AGENTS.md`