  "tool_info": {
    "response": "*Rules used for this response:*\n- (Always On) Triggered Rule: AGENTS.md\n\n### Planner Response\n\nI\u2019ll draft several **worktree-ready prompts** that fit your slide/demo framing and this repo. I\u2019ll keep them independent enough to run in parallel, especially around the `demos-coghealth-ehr-web` frontend per your project rules.## Expanded Git Worktrees Demo Prompts\n\nHere are several **worktree-worthy prompts** you can run in parallel across separate Cascade sessions. They are intentionally scoped as independent UI/API/test tasks so they demonstrate why worktrees are useful.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Git Worktrees \u2014 SLIDE + DEMO\n\n### Slide Talk Track\n\n> \u201cGit Worktrees solve parallelization for AI agents. You can spin up multiple Cascade sessions, each on a different worktree \u2014 a separate working copy of your repo. They run in parallel without conflicts.\u201d\n\n> \u201cComposability means you can run and merge agentic changes that might otherwise conflict. When a session finishes, you click **Merge** to incorporate the changes back into your main workspace.\u201d\n\n> \u201cThis lets you treat agents like parallel teammates. One can build a dashboard feature, another can improve a patient detail view, another can add test coverage \u2014 all without stepping on each other\u2019s files.\u201d\n\n---\n\n# Prompt 4 \u2014 Worktree-worthy Independent Task\n\n## Mode\n\n**Code**  \nSpin up a new worktree if available, otherwise just demo the prompt.\n\n## Prompt\n\n```text\nAdd a small \"Quality Metrics\" card to the right sidebar of the dashboard showing 3 hardcoded stats:\n\n- Notes signed on time: 92%\n- Orders completed: 87%\n- Patient satisfaction: 4.6/5\n\nUse the existing ehr-panel styling and preserve the current EHR desktop look. Keep the change compact and focused. Do not add dependencies.\n\nAfter the change, validate with:\n\nnpm run build\n\nfrom the demos-coghealth-ehr-web directory.\n```\n\n## Talk Track\n\n> \u201cThis is exactly the kind of task you'd spin off into a worktree \u2014 an independent sidebar feature that doesn't conflict with other work. Cascade builds it, you review it, click merge, done.\u201d\n\n> \u201cIf I had two more features to build, I could spin up two more worktrees and have them all running simultaneously.\u201d\n\n---\n\n# Prompt 5 \u2014 Parallel Dashboard Enhancement\n\n## Mode\n\n**Code**  \nUse a separate worktree.\n\n## Prompt\n\n```text\nAdd a compact \"Care Gaps\" card to the dashboard using existing EHR panel/card styling.\n\nThe card should show 3 hardcoded care gaps:\n\n- A1C overdue\n- Annual wellness visit due\n- Medication reconciliation needed\n\nUse lucide-react icons only if they are already used in the app. Keep the design consistent with the existing dashboard and avoid broad rewrites.\n\nValidate with:\n\nnpm run build\n\nfrom demos-coghealth-ehr-web.\n```\n\n## Why This Is Worktree-Worthy\n\n- **Independent surface area**: Another dashboard card.\n- **Low merge risk**: Can be reviewed and merged separately.\n- **Good agent task**: Clear design constraints and bounded scope.\n\n## Talk Track\n\n> \u201cHere\u2019s another parallelizable unit of work. It touches a small, isolated part of the dashboard. Another agent can build this while the first agent works on Quality Metrics.\u201d\n\n---\n\n# Prompt 6 \u2014 Patient Detail UI Improvement\n\n## Mode\n\n**Code**  \nUse a separate worktree.\n\n## Prompt\n\n```text\nImprove the patient detail page by adding a compact \"Risk Summary\" section.\n\nShow 3 hardcoded risk indicators:\n\n- Fall risk: Moderate\n- Readmission risk: Low\n- Medication complexity: High\n\nUse the existing EHR visual style, Tailwind patterns, and compact panel layout. Do not change backend APIs. Do not add dependencies.\n\nValidate with:\n\nnpm run build\n\nfrom demos-coghealth-ehr-web.\n```\n\n## Why This Is Worktree-Worthy\n\n- **Different feature area**: Patient detail instead of dashboard.\n- **Safe parallelization**: Less likely to conflict with dashboard work.\n- **Easy review**: Visual change is isolated and demo-friendly.\n\n## Talk Track\n\n> \u201cThis worktree is focused on patient detail, while the others are focused on dashboard widgets. That separation is exactly why worktrees are useful.\u201d\n\n---\n\n# Prompt 7 \u2014 Small Test Coverage Task\n\n## Mode\n\n**Code**  \nUse a separate worktree.\n\n## Prompt\n\n```text\nAdd or improve frontend test coverage for the dashboard.\n\nFocus on verifying that key dashboard sections render successfully. Keep the tests aligned with the existing test style in the demos-coghealth-ehr-web/tests directory.\n\nDo not rewrite the test setup. Do not add new dependencies unless absolutely required.\n\nRun the relevant test command if available, and also validate with:\n\nnpm run build\n\nfrom demos-coghealth-ehr-web.\n```\n\n## Why This Is Worktree-Worthy\n\n- **Different kind of work**: Testing instead of UI implementation.\n- **Can run in parallel**: Doesn\u2019t block feature agents.\n- **Useful merge candidate**: Strengthens confidence before merging UI changes.\n\n## Talk Track\n\n> \u201cNot every worktree has to build a new feature. One agent can add test coverage while others implement UI changes. That gives you both velocity and confidence.\u201d\n\n---\n\n# Prompt 8 \u2014 Backend/API Investigation or Cleanup\n\n## Mode\n\n**Code**  \nUse a separate worktree.\n\n## Prompt\n\n```text\nReview the patient service layer in the backend and identify one small, safe cleanup opportunity.\n\nIf there is a low-risk improvement, implement it. Examples could include simplifying duplicated logic, improving a method name, or tightening null/error handling.\n\nDo not change API behavior. Do not make broad architectural changes. Keep the diff small and explain the before/after.\n\nRun the relevant backend validation command if available.\n```\n\n## Why This Is Worktree-Worthy\n\n- **Separate repo area**: Backend service code instead of frontend.\n- **Low conflict risk**: Can proceed independently from UI work.\n- **Good review exercise**: Demonstrates that worktrees can support exploratory agent tasks.\n\n## Talk Track\n\n> \u201cThis is a different type of agent job: a backend cleanup or investigation. It runs separately from the frontend worktrees, and I can decide later whether it\u2019s worth merging.\u201d\n\n---\n\n# Prompt 9 \u2014 UI Polish Pass\n\n## Mode\n\n**Code**  \nUse a separate worktree.\n\n## Prompt\n\n```text\nDo a small UI polish pass on the dashboard.\n\nLook for one or two compact improvements that preserve the existing EHR desktop style. Examples:\n\n- Better spacing between dashboard panels\n- Slightly clearer section headings\n- More consistent muted text sizing\n- Better alignment of sidebar elements\n\nKeep the diff small. Avoid broad rewrites. Do not add dependencies.\n\nValidate with:\n\nnpm run build\n\nfrom demos-coghealth-ehr-web.\n```\n\n## Why This Is Worktree-Worthy\n\n- **Exploratory but bounded**: Lets the agent improve visible quality.\n- **Can be accepted or discarded**: Perfect worktree use case.\n- **Non-blocking**: Doesn\u2019t interfere with core feature work.\n\n## Talk Track\n\n> \u201cA polish pass is a great worktree candidate because I might like it, or I might throw it away. Worktrees make that experimentation cheap.\u201d\n\n---\n\n# Prompt 10 \u2014 Accessibility Pass\n\n## Mode\n\n**Code**  \nUse a separate worktree.\n\n## Prompt\n\n```text\nPerform a small accessibility pass on the frontend dashboard.\n\nLook for obvious issues such as missing button labels, weak semantic structure, or unclear interactive affordances. Make only small, safe improvements.\n\nDo not redesign the page. Do not add dependencies. Preserve the existing EHR desktop style.\n\nValidate with:\n\nnpm run build\n\nfrom demos-coghealth-ehr-web.\n```\n\n## Why This Is Worktree-Worthy\n\n- **Independent quality improvement**: Not tied to a specific feature.\n- **Easy to review separately**: Accessibility changes can be evaluated on their own.\n- **Useful demo of agent specialization**: One agent builds, another improves quality.\n\n## Talk Track\n\n> \u201cThis shows composability beyond feature work. One worktree can build a feature, another can improve accessibility, another can add tests.\u201d\n\n---\n\n# Suggested Live Demo Sequence\n\n## Step 1 \u2014 Start with Prompt 4\n\nUse the **Quality Metrics** card because it is visual, bounded, and easy to explain.\n\n## Step 2 \u2014 Spin up two more worktrees\n\nRun these in parallel:\n\n- **Prompt 5**: Add `Care Gaps` dashboard card.\n- **Prompt 7**: Add dashboard test coverage.\n\n## Step 3 \u2014 Explain the merge flow\n\n> \u201cEach Cascade session has its own working copy. They don\u2019t fight over the same local changes. When a task finishes, I inspect the diff and click **Merge**.\u201d\n\n## Step 4 \u2014 Reinforce the principle\n\n> \u201cThe point isn\u2019t just parallel coding. It\u2019s parallel experimentation. I can merge the useful work, discard what I don\u2019t like, and keep my main workspace clean.\u201d\n\n---\n\n# Best Prompts for the Live Workshop\n\nIf you only want **three** prompts, I\u2019d use:\n\n- **Prompt 4**: Quality Metrics card\n- **Prompt 5**: Care Gaps card\n- **Prompt 7**: Dashboard test coverage\n\nThese show the clearest parallelization story:\n\n- **Feature A**: Right sidebar metric card\n- **Feature B**: Another independent dashboard card\n- **Quality lane**: Test coverage\n\n**Status:** Drafted multiple worktree-ready prompts and talk tracks for expanding the Git Worktrees slide/demo."
