[2026-05-13T22:08:55.586197-07:00] post_cascade_response
  Full data: {
  "agent_action_name": "post_cascade_response",
  "trajectory_id": "63893a4d-97d6-4a62-9e13-ede1f0c1812a",
  "timestamp": "2026-05-13T22:08:55.586197-07:00",
  "execution_id": "26997a41-ce17-41ed-b4b1-482d72e41dd6",
  "model_name": "GPT-5.5 Low Thinking",
  "tool_info": {
    "response": "*Rules used for this response:*\n- (Always On) Triggered Rule: AGENTS.md\n\n### Planner Response\n\nA **git worktree** is like having multiple checkout folders for the same Git repository.\n\nNormally, one repo folder can only have one branch checked out at a time. With worktrees, you can have:\n\n- **Main folder**: `main` branch\n- **Separate folder**: feature branch\n- **Another folder**: bugfix branch\n\nAll of them share the same underlying Git history, but each folder has its own files checked out.\n\n## Simple analogy\n\nThink of your repo as a book.\n\nA normal Git checkout lets you open the book to one page at a time.\n\nA git worktree gives you multiple desks, each with the same book opened to a different page. You can work on different chapters without constantly flipping back and forth.\n\n## Why it is valuable\n\n- **Work on multiple branches at once**  \n  You can keep one branch running while editing another.\n\n- **Avoid constant branch switching**  \n  No need to stash changes just to check something on another branch.\n\n- **Safer experiments**  \n  Try risky changes in a separate folder without disturbing your main working copy.\n\n- **Better for parallel tasks**  \n  Useful when reviewing a PR, fixing a bug, and building a feature at the same time.\n\n- **Cleaner context**  \n  Each task gets its own directory, so files and uncommitted changes do not get mixed together.\n\n## Tiny example\n\n```bash\ngit worktree add ../my-feature feature-branch\n```\n\nThat creates a new folder `../my-feature` checked out to `feature-branch`.\n\nYour original repo stays exactly where it is, on whatever branch it was using.\n\n## Summary\n\nA **git worktree** is a separate working folder for another branch of the same repo. It is valuable because it lets you work on multiple tasks independently without stashing, switching branches, or mixing changes. \n```\n        main repo folder     feature worktree      bugfix worktree\n        branch: main         branch: feature       branch: bugfix\n``` \n        main repo folder     feature worktree      bugfix worktree\n        branch: main         branch: feature       branch: bugfix\n```\n```\n        main repo folder     feature worktree      bugfix worktree\n        branch: main         branch: feature       branch: bugfix\n``` \n```\n        main repo folder     feature worktree      bugfix worktree\n        branch: main         branch: feature       branch: bugfix\n``` \n```\n        main repo folder     feature worktree      bugfix worktree\n        branch: main         branch: feature       branch: bugfix\n``` \nA **git worktree** is a separate working folder for the same Git repository.\n\nIt lets you check out different branches at the same time in different folders.\n\n## Simple example\n\nInstead of constantly switching your repo between branches:\n\n```text\nrepo/\n  branch: main\n```\n\nYou can have:\n\n```text\nrepo/\n  branch: main\n\nrepo-feature/\n  branch: new-dashboard\n\nrepo-bugfix/\n  branch: fix-login\n```\n\nThey all share the same Git history, but each folder has its own checked-out files.\n\n## Why it is valuable\n\n- **No constant branch switching**  \n  You do not need to stash or commit half-finished work just to look at another branch.\n\n- **Parallel work is easier**  \n  You can build a feature, review another branch, and fix a bug in separate folders.\n\n- **Cleaner changes**  \n  Each task stays isolated, so files from one task do not get mixed with another.\n\n- **Safer experimentation**  \n  You can try risky changes in a separate worktree without disturbing your main folder.\n\n## In one sentence\n\nA **git worktree** gives you multiple working copies of one repo, so you can work on multiple branches independently without juggling or mixing changes."
  }
}
