    "response": "*Rules used for this response:*\n- (Always On) Triggered Rule: AGENTS.md\n\n### Planner Response\n\nI\u2019ll explain how this Spring Security `userdetails` context relates to your app\u2019s authentication flow, especially the open `CustomUserDetailsService.loadUserByUsername` usage.## Context: `org.springframework.security.core.userdetails`\n\n`org.springframework.security.core.userdetails` is a Spring Security package that defines the core contract for loading authenticated users.\n\nIn your app, this package is the bridge between:\n\n- **Your domain user model**: likely `com.medchart.ehr.domain.User`\n- **Your persistence layer**: likely `UserRepository`\n- **Spring Security authentication**: login, JWT validation, and authorization\n\nThe important pieces are:\n\n- **`UserDetailsService`**: service interface Spring Security calls to load a user.\n- **`UserDetails`**: standard interface representing an authenticated user.\n- **`UsernameNotFoundException`**: exception thrown when lookup fails.\n\n## Why `UsernameNotFoundException` matters\n\n`UsernameNotFoundException` is a specialized authentication exception.\n\nIt is thrown when Spring Security asks your app:\n\n```java\nloadUserByUsername(String username)\n```\n\nand your application cannot find a matching user.\n\nIn your `CustomUserDetailsService`, the method likely looks up a user by username or email. If the repository returns empty, it throws `UsernameNotFoundException`.\n\nConceptually:\n\n```java\nreturn userRepository.findByUsername(username)\n    .orElseThrow(() -> new UsernameNotFoundException(\"User not found\"));\n```\n\nThis tells Spring Security:\n\n- **The credentials cannot be validated**\n- **Authentication should fail**\n- **The request should not continue as an authenticated user**\n\n## Role in the authentication flow\n\nA typical flow in this project is probably:\n\n1. **User submits credentials**\n   - Example: username/password login endpoint.\n\n2. **Spring Security calls `CustomUserDetailsService`**\n   - It invokes `loadUserByUsername(username)`.\n\n3. **Your repository loads the user**\n   - Usually from the database using `UserRepository`.\n\n4. **Your `User` entity is returned as `UserDetails`**\n   - This works if your `User` class implements Spring Security\u2019s `UserDetails`.\n\n5. **Spring Security validates credentials**\n   - It compares the submitted password against the stored encoded password.\n\n6. **If successful, JWT/session is issued**\n   - In your app, JWT-related classes likely consume `UserDetails`.\n\n7. **If user is missing, `UsernameNotFoundException` is thrown**\n   - Authentication fails cleanly.\n\n## Why your domain `User` can work with Spring Security\n\nSpring Security does not know your database schema. It only knows the `UserDetails` interface.\n\nSo your application adapts its own `User` entity by implementing methods like:\n\n- `getUsername()`\n- `getPassword()`\n- `getAuthorities()`\n- `isAccountNonExpired()`\n- `isAccountNonLocked()`\n- `isCredentialsNonExpired()`\n- `isEnabled()`\n\nThat lets Spring Security treat your app-specific `User` as a security user.\n\n## How this relates to JWT classes\n\nClasses like `JwtTokenProvider` and `JwtAuthenticationFilter` typically use `UserDetails` after parsing a token.\n\nA common JWT request flow is:\n\n1. Request includes `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.\n2. JWT filter extracts the username from the token.\n3. JWT filter calls `UserDetailsService.loadUserByUsername(username)`.\n4. If user exists and token is valid, Spring Security creates an authenticated context.\n5. If not, authentication fails.\n\nSo `UsernameNotFoundException` is relevant not only during login, but also during token-based request authentication.\n\n## Key idea\n\n`UsernameNotFoundException` is not just a generic error. It is part of Spring Security\u2019s expected authentication contract.\n\nIt means:\n\n- **The requested principal does not exist**\n- **Spring Security should reject authentication**\n- **The failure can be handled by Spring\u2019s authentication failure mechanisms**\n\n## Practical implication for your code\n\nIn `CustomUserDetailsService.loadUserByUsername`, throwing `UsernameNotFoundException` is the correct behavior when no matching user is found.\n\nIt keeps the service compliant with Spring Security\u2019s `UserDetailsService` contract and allows authentication failures to be handled consistently.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `org.springframework.security.core.userdetails` package provides the standard Spring Security interfaces your app uses to authenticate users. Your `CustomUserDetailsService` loads your application\u2019s `User` from storage, returns it as `UserDetails`, and throws `UsernameNotFoundException` when lookup fails. This is central to both password login and JWT request validation."
  }
