  "tool_info": {
    "response": "I found several issues in the `PromptDashboard` component:\n\n## Bugs & Logic Errors\n\n1. **Misleading \"Save Draft\" behavior**\n   - **Problem**: The \"Save Draft\" button only moves text to the status panel (`lastSubmittedPrompt`) without actual persistence (no localStorage, API call, or parent callback). This creates false expectations.\n   - **Impact**: Users think they're saving but data is lost on refresh.\n   - **Fix**: Either remove the button, implement real persistence (localStorage), or expose an `onSaveDraft` prop.\n\n2. **Reset clears submission history unintentionally**\n   - **Problem**: `handleReset` clears both the current prompt AND `lastSubmittedPrompt`. Users might want to clear the draft while keeping submission history.\n   - **Impact**: Loss of reference to what was previously submitted.\n   - **Fix**: Split into separate actions or make reset behavior explicit (e.g., confirm dialog).\n\n3. **Whitespace-only input edge case**\n   - **Problem**: Button disabled with `!prompt.trim()` but submission uses `prompt.trim()`. If user types only spaces, button stays disabled (correct), but UX is confusing since they see content in textarea.\n   - **Impact**: Minor UX confusion.\n   - **Fix**: Consider visual indicator (e.g., warning) or auto-trim on blur.\n\n## Security Vulnerabilities\n\n4. **No input length limits**\n   - **Problem**: No maximum length constraint on prompt input. Extremely long prompts could cause memory issues or DOS if sent to an API.\n   - **Impact**: Potential resource exhaustion, especially if prompts are sent to external services.\n   - **Fix**: Add `maxLength` prop (e.g., 10,000 characters) and validation.\n\n5. **XSS risk if used with untrusted data**\n   - **Problem**: While React escapes by default, `{lastSubmittedPrompt}` renders directly. If this component ever receives data from external sources, it could be XSS.\n   - **Impact**: Currently safe (local state only), but fragile pattern.\n   - **Fix**: Document that component expects trusted input only, or sanitize if data source changes.\n\n## Performance Issues\n\n6. **No debouncing on parent callback**\n   - **Problem**: If `onSubmitPrompt` triggers expensive operations (API calls, complex processing), there's no debouncing or rate limiting.\n   - **Impact**: Could cause duplicate submissions or performance issues.\n   - **Fix**: Add loading state and disable button during submission; consider debouncing if auto-submit is ever added.\n\n7. **Unnecessary re-renders on character count**\n   - **Problem**: Every keystroke triggers re-render of character count and status text. Minimal impact for small component, but not optimal.\n   - **Impact**: Negligible here, but bad pattern to copy.\n   - **Fix**: Acceptable for this use case; could optimize with `useMemo` if component grows.\n\n## Missing Error Handling\n\n8. **No error handling for parent callback**\n   - **Problem**: No try/catch around `onSubmitPrompt?.(trimmedPrompt)`. If parent throws, component crashes.\n   - **Impact**: Poor error recovery; user sees broken UI.\n   - **Fix**: Wrap in try/catch and show error message to user.\n\n9. **No loading/feedback state**\n   - **Problem**: If `onSubmitPrompt` is async, no visual indication that submission is in progress. User could submit multiple times.\n   - **Impact**: Duplicate submissions, poor UX.\n   - **Fix**: Add `isSubmitting` state, disable button during submission, show loading indicator.\n\n10. **No success/failure feedback**\n    - **Problem**: After submission, no indication of success or failure unless parent provides it.\n    - **Impact**: User doesn't know if prompt was processed.\n    - **Fix**: Accept `onSubmitPrompt` return value or add callback prop for status updates.\n\n11. **No validation for minimum length**\n    - **Problem**: Only checks for empty string after trim. No minimum meaningful length (e.g., 10 chars).\n    - **Impact**: Could submit meaningless single-character prompts.\n    - **Fix**: Add minimum length validation with error message.\n\n## Code Style & Convention Violations\n\n12. **Hardcoded background color**\n    - **Problem**: Uses `bg-[#ece9d8]` hardcoded instead of relying on existing CSS variables or theme. Other parts of the app use `background: #d4d0c8` (slightly different).\n    - **Impact**: Inconsistent theming; harder to maintain.\n    - **Fix**: Use existing CSS variable or remove inline style to let parent control background.\n\n13. **Mixed styling paradigms**\n    - **Problem**: Uses Tailwind `text-blue-100` for subtitle but custom `ehr-*` classes elsewhere. Inconsistent with project's EHR desktop style.\n    - **Impact**: Visual inconsistency; harder to maintain theme.\n    - **Fix**: Use `ehr-*` classes or CSS variables instead of arbitrary Tailwind colors.\n\n14. **Missing TypeScript exports**\n    - **Problem**: `PromptDashboardProps` interface is not exported, making it harder for consumers to use the component with proper typing.\n    - **Impact**: Poor developer experience for TypeScript users.\n    - **Fix**: Export the interface: `export interface PromptDashboardProps`.\n\n15. **No documentation**\n    - **Problem**: No JSDoc or comments explaining component purpose, props, or usage.\n    - **Impact**: Harder for other developers to understand and use correctly.\n    - **Fix**: Add JSDoc comment with description, props, and usage example.\n\n16. **Assumes specific parent layout**\n    - **Problem**: Uses `h-full` which requires parent to have defined height. If used without constrained height, layout breaks.\n    - **Impact**: Component may not work in all contexts.\n    - **Fix**: Document height requirement or make height configurable via prop.\n\n17. **Textarea resize disabled unnecessarily**\n    - **Problem**: `resize-none` prevents user from expanding textarea, but component uses `flex-1` which should handle this. Restrictive UX.\n    - **Impact**: Users cannot adjust textarea size if needed.\n    - **Fix**: Remove `resize-none` or make it configurable.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe component has **17 issues** ranging from misleading UX (save draft doesn't save) to missing error handling, security concerns (no length limits), and style inconsistencies. The most critical fixes needed are:\n\n1. Fix or remove the misleading \"Save Draft\" button\n2. Add error handling and loading state for submissions\n3. Add input length limits\n4. Fix styling inconsistencies (remove hardcoded colors, use ehr-* classes)\n5. Export TypeScript interface and add documentation\n\nThe component follows the basic EHR style pattern correctly but needs refinement before production use."
  }
}
